---
description: Production-grade frontend engineering for Next.js and TypeScript against a Django/DRF backend — plan first, verify the installed versions, never invent an API, keep the diff minimal, run the checks, and hold the work to a stated definition of done. Covers routing and rendering, the server/client boundary, the React component tree, the typed backend contract, and the non-functional guarantees (accessibility, security, performance, testing) that gate completion. Domains are integrated one per release; the router in SKILL.md is the authoritative list of what is loadable. Canonical instructions live in SKILL.md and references/.
globs: ["**/*.ts", "**/*.tsx"]
alwaysApply: false
---

When writing or reviewing frontend code in this repo, use the skill defined in
`SKILL.md` at the repo root as the source of truth. Read `SKILL.md` first for
the standing rules, the router, and the mode logic, then open the relevant
`references/*.md` file(s). `AGENTS.md` is the condensed always-on layer and
defers to `SKILL.md` on any conflict. Review-time produces findings ordered by
severity (location, the concrete failure a user would experience, fix) and
ends by stating what was not reviewed; write-time plans first, then applies
the defaults from the integrated domains while generating code.

Treat these as first-class triggers regardless of the wording of the task:
anything under `app/`, a route handler, a layout or page, `proxy.ts`,
`next.config.ts`, `tsconfig.json`, or `package.json`; a `"use client"` or
`"use server"` boundary; a React hook or a React 19 API; a compiler flag, a
cast, an `any`, a union, a branded id, or a Zod schema; a response type that
came from a DRF endpoint; an interactive control, a form, or a focus order;
and any change to the bundle, the headers, or the build. Verify the installed
versions from `package.json` before generating code — never mix Next 15 and
Next 16 idioms in one file — and report an API you cannot confirm as
unconfirmed rather than guessing at it.

Work is not done because it renders: typecheck, lint, the tests covering the
change, and the production build all have to be run. Apply the conflict rule
— security > accessibility > correctness > performance > developer convenience
— with no level trading down to satisfy a level above it. The domain routing
table grows one entry per release. Do not rely on this summary alone — read
the referenced files.
